# candor-swift self-gate (SPEC §7.12): the analyzer reads source and writes reports — Fs/Env are its
# job. It must never reach the network or a database — anywhere, ever.
#
# It spawns a subprocess in exactly one FILE, main.swift, and both uses are "run a tool again": the
# `--workspace` chain spawns candor-swift itself on each workspace dependency and reads the report back
# through a Pipe (Exec + Ipc), and Xcode-scope resolution shells out via `/usr/bin/env` to resolve a
# target. Those are the feature, not a hidden effect.
#
# A prefix deny cannot say "deny Exec everywhere except main.swift", so `ci/self-gate.sh` proves the
# two halves separately (candor-java and candor-ts split the same problem the same way):
#   (1) the WHOLE engine under THIS policy — no Net, no Db, with no file excluded;
#   (2) the Exec/Ipc-performing units are EXACTLY the four declared in that script — so a new
#       subprocess anywhere in the engine fails the gate, main.swift included.
#
# WHY `deny Net Db` AND NOT `deny Net Db Exec Ipc`. The stricter string used to live here, and CI made
# it pass by COPYING Sources to a temp dir and DELETING main.swift first — carving the exemption out a
# whole FILE at a time and leaving 2158 lines where a new subprocess was caught by nothing. The weaker
# rule over the WHOLE tree, plus a unit-level declaration, is the stronger gate. A policy is only as
# strong as the scope it is actually evaluated over.
deny Net Db
